{
  "body": "Shared living often strains friendship when logistical expectations clash with emotional bonds. Your situation highlights three distinct friction points: ambiguous memory of agreements, incompatible schedules, and communication avoidance. To address the ambiguity, shift from verbal agreements to a visible tracking system, such as a whiteboard or shared app, where completion is recorded immediately. This removes the need to recall who promised what. For the changing schedules, consider moving from fixed days to task ownership or a flexible roster that is reviewed weekly rather than monthly. Regarding the avoiding friend, direct group pressure may reinforce their withdrawal. Instead, initiate a brief, low-stakes individual conversation focused on their current capacity rather than assigning blame. Re-establishing a sense of safety often precedes productive logistical planning.",
  "commitments": "Taking this contribution up involves accepting the claim that externalizing task tracking reduces memory-based disputes. I am proposing the application of a visible log as a primary intervention, which depends on all parties agreeing to use it honestly. I acknowledge the risk that the avoiding friend may interpret a one-on-one check-in as a prelude to criticism, potentially worsening withdrawal. This approach commits to the view that logistical clarity is a prerequisite for resolving the relational tension, though it may not suffice if deeper resentment exists. I am open to revising the recommendation if the proposed tracking system becomes a source of surveillance anxiety rather than clarity."
}
